#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068428 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "607443d5-b1dd-56b1-919b-fcc652ac3bc9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Function Value is Not Defined"}] :question/id #uuid "282add39-5184-589e-bb7b-b87a59cd41e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/eead78a813f820cf12cbd05bd22b230b1753c7e4ebe1e77d2c1eb6b61c11f4ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5fe1482-00d6-5738-b56a-8c082912d8d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Limit is Not Equal to the Function Value"}] :question/id #uuid "fb401fbe-a718-5ea7-b598-139ef837b047" :question/math-academy-id "e-2" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0} f(x)$ for the function below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0ed78e2e640611d70263e17a76eb4a13e9e2816efba02494b53e540d38065d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $0,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)= 1\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(0)$ is $2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "851c841f-6e27-5f81-8a0d-0f2439ae3e61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "ab95d805-958c-5de3-99bd-41368cc57080" :question/math-academy-id "e-3" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below? \n\nI. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0$\n\nIII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)$\n\n![](images/68/68241f3cd913cd91d10765499dc4ce4398fb3e8a81ec4bcfb65b47c0d77731d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $3,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,3}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSimiliarly, as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)\\ne\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}]}
